*

!fetch_cvpr2023_models.sh
!fetch_grasp_trigger_PRE_2.sh

# grasp pin tables (per-split OMG grasp selections — small JSON, useful to version)
!grasp_pin_table_train.json
!grasp_pin_table_val.json

# Phase-5 grasp tables and exclusion lists. Same reasoning, with more force: the
# final table is what says WHICH FOUR grasps a run committed to, and a Phase-5
# log cannot be interpreted without it. It is also expensive to regenerate — it
# is the output of the K=8 collection, not of a planner sweep — and the .h5 it
# was derived from lives on /scratch and is never committed. A few hundred KB.
!grasp_cand_table_*_regrasp.json
!bc_dataset/
!bc_dataset/*_regrasp.json
!bc_dataset/*_regrasp_excluded.json

# REGRASP tables. Same reasoning again, and it bit: none of these matched a
# whitelist above, so a fresh checkout on the cluster had NO tables at all and
# the pipeline stopped at its preflight. ~3 MB for all of them.
#
# `direction_table_*` is a GPU sim pass (~20 min/split) and is the input every
# later stage is derived from. `regrasp_pins_*` is what says WHICH direction a
# run committed to per scene, so a dagger_log cannot be interpreted without it.
# `regrasp_demos_*_ok.json` records which (scene, bin) pairs the collection
# actually managed — it is the difference between the table and the data, and
# the .h5 it came from lives on /scratch and is never committed.
!direction_table_*.json
!direction_feasibility_*.json
!regrasp_pins_*.json
!regrasp_demos_*_ok.json

# Per-(scene, grasp) demonstration outcomes (examples/build_demo_table.py).
# Tracked for the same reason as the tables above and one more: this is the only
# record of WHICH demonstrations actually reached their grasp, and the .h5 it is
# derived from lives on /scratch and is never committed. Losing the shard loses
# the ability to recompute it. 7 KB for the matrix, ~100 KB for the per-episode
# detail; a directory rule is needed because `*` on line 5 ignores the folder
# itself before any file pattern inside it can match.
!bc_dataset/tables/
!bc_dataset/tables/*.csv

# Track lightweight per-run metadata under rl_runs/ (training logs, plots, and
# configs) so runs can be committed/pushed for record-keeping. The parent dirs
# must be re-included first, then the specific files. The large checkpoints/
# (100s of MB of .pt weights) stay ignored — do NOT commit those to git.
!rl_runs/
!rl_runs/*/
!rl_runs/*/*.csv
!rl_runs/*/*.yaml
!rl_runs/*/*.npz
rl_runs/*/checkpoints/

# Same policy for dagger_runs/ — track the lightweight per-run metadata (log,
# config, state, grasp registry) but NOT the large per-iter checkpoints and demo
# data (best/ last/ iters/ data/ — ~1 GB/run). Only files DIRECTLY in the run
# dir are re-included; the heavy subdirs stay ignored (one level deeper).
#
# *.png is deliberately NOT re-included here (nor under rl_runs/ above).
# curves.png, curves_diag.png and scene_eval_*.png are DERIVED — they replot the
# dagger_log.csv / scene_eval_*.csv that ARE tracked, via plot_dagger_run.py and
# eval_run_scenes.py — and they were 32 MB across 84 files, by far the largest
# thing in the repo. Regenerate rather than store. Contrast normalization.npz
# (~1.3 KB), re-included below: the checkpoint payload does NOT carry the
# Normalizer and the .h5 it was fitted on lives on /scratch and is never
# committed, so the .npz is the only surviving record of how to denormalize a
# policy's output. That one is primary, not derived.
!dagger_runs/
!dagger_runs/*/
!dagger_runs/*/*.csv
!dagger_runs/*/*.json
!dagger_runs/*/*.yaml
!dagger_runs/*/*.npz
# per-iter logs/metadata (dagger_runs/*/iters/iter_NN/) — track the small
# log.csv/config/normalization, but the per-iter checkpoints/ (21 MB .pt each)
# stay ignored (one level deeper, not re-included).
!dagger_runs/*/iters/
!dagger_runs/*/iters/*/
!dagger_runs/*/iters/*/*.csv
!dagger_runs/*/iters/*/*.yaml
!dagger_runs/*/iters/*/*.npz
